-Limen records apartment handovers on an Android phone or an iPhone. It is published by Alexis Peringer. Both versions record the same protocol and produce the same PDF, and this notice covers them both. Where the two platforms differ, the text says which is which.
+Limen records apartment handovers on Android and iPhone. It is published by Alexis Peringer. This notice covers both platforms and identifies the version-specific differences.
+
+## Android 1.2
+
+Android 1.2 can also store supporting documents, individual participant positions and follow-up notes. It retains exact exported PDFs and accessible HTML companions in private storage. Recovery copies can retain removed evidence until the complete record is deleted. Exports in Downloads and copies shared with other apps must be deleted separately. A backup can cover one record or the library and contains the referenced evidence and retained exports. Creating an unencrypted archive requires an explicit choice.
+
+## iPhone 1.2
+
+iPhone 1.2 also records supporting documents, each participant's position and follow-up notes. Limen keeps exact exported PDF and HTML copies in private storage so the user can reopen them. At the user's request, it saves the finished copies in Documents/Limen, visible in Files under On My iPhone, and offers the system share sheet. Deleting a record removes its private evidence, recovery copies and retained exports; it does not delete copies already exported or shared.
+
+Recovery copies can still refer to removed photos, signature ink or supporting documents. Limen therefore retains these originals privately until the complete record is deleted. Recovery also preserves an unreadable record's original file. A backup can contain one record or the library, with each selected record's referenced evidence and retained exports. It excludes unused originals and private recovery copies. An unprotected ZIP requires an explicit choice and is readable by anyone who has the file. Password-protected archives are encrypted; Limen cannot recover a lost password.
+
+The optional device-owner authentication gate protects entry to the saved-record library and settings. It leaves the current shared handover accessible and does not encrypt exported files. The user can enable it only after successful device authentication.
 
 ## The short version
 
 > Peringer does not collect, receive, sell or share the personal data entered into Limen. There is no Limen account, developer-operated server, advertising, analytics, telemetry or crash-reporting service. Protocols, photos and drawn signatures stay in the app's private storage unless the user exports, shares or backs them up.
 
 The person or organisation using a Limen installation decides why and how protocol data is recorded and is the data controller for that data. A professional operator remains responsible for giving participants its identity, contact details and any additional notice its processing requires.
+
+For access, correction or deletion requests concerning a handover, contact the person or organisation identified as the data controller in the participant notice. That operator manages the record and any copies it has shared. Peringer cannot access records stored on the user's device.
 
 ## Data the user can record
 
@@ -25,13 +41,13 @@
 
 Limen can create a backup containing records, original photos and signature ink, and saves it where it saves the PDF: Downloads on Android, the Limen folder in Documents on iPhone. A password on that backup is optional. With one, the archive is encrypted and Limen cannot recover the password. Without one, anyone who has the file can read everything in it, which the app says on screen when it saves. A backup in either place is outside Limen's private storage and under the user's control. Restoring a backup reads only the file the user selects.
 
-While it builds a password-protected archive, the iPhone version writes the unencrypted intermediate copy into its own cache rather than beside the finished file, removes it when the export ends, and sweeps it again on the next export in case the app was killed part way.
+On iPhone 1.2, Limen stages exports privately before publishing the finished files to Documents. It removes temporary files when an operation ends and clears abandoned staging when the app next starts. An encrypted backup can have an unencrypted intermediate copy in this private staging area.
 
-Limen keeps its private app data out of the platform's own backups. On Android it disables cloud backup and device-to-device transfer. On iPhone it marks those files as excluded, so neither an iCloud backup nor a backup to a computer copies them.
+On Android, Limen disables cloud backup and device-to-device transfer. On iPhone 1.2, Limen excludes private records, original evidence, recovery copies, retained PDF/HTML copies and private operation staging from system backups. iOS Data Protection protects those private files after a restart until the first device unlock; this is separate from password-protected archive encryption. Preferences remain subject to the operating system's normal storage and backup behaviour. They can include operator/contact defaults, interface choices, the open record identifier and recovery values for recent input. Deleting a record does not clear reusable contact defaults; the operator must also remove those defaults when appropriate. Files exported to Documents or shared with another app are separate copies under the user's control.
 
 ## Network use
 
-Limen uses the network for one in-app service. After the user types at least three characters in a Swiss address field, the app waits briefly and automatically sends the typed search text and ordinary network metadata to the Swiss federal swisstopo SearchServer at `https://api3.geo.admin.ch/rest/services/ech/SearchServer`. That service is operated under its own terms and privacy information. The lookup does not send protocol records, photos or signatures to Peringer. On iPhone the app makes that request through an ephemeral session, so neither the typed address nor the reply is written to a shared cache on disk.
+On Android 1.2 and iPhone 1.2, online address suggestions are optional and off by default. Before activation, Limen identifies swisstopo and explains that the typed address is sent; the user confirms the choice. When enabled, typing at least three characters in a Swiss address field sends the search text and ordinary network metadata to the federal swisstopo SearchServer at `https://api3.geo.admin.ch/rest/services/ech/SearchServer`. Manual entry works offline. Earlier Android and iPhone versions start suggestions automatically after typing. Swisstopo operates under its own terms and privacy information. The lookup does not send records, photos or signatures to Peringer. On iPhone, an ephemeral network session avoids a shared disk cache for the query and reply.
 
 The app also contains links to independent rental-deposit providers. Opening one hands the URL to the user's browser; the browser and destination site then operate under their own terms. Limen does not receive information about the visit.
 
@@ -39,13 +55,13 @@
 
 On Android, Limen declares no camera or storage permission: a photo is taken by the device's system camera through a temporary grant for one destination file, or selected through the system picker.
 
-On iPhone, the camera is the one permission Limen asks for, because iOS has no way to hand the capture to a separate camera app the way Android does. The photo library is reached through the system picker, which needs no permission. In both versions, only the system components involved and the item the user chooses take part.
+On iPhone, Limen asks for camera access to take a photo inside the app. The system photo picker gives access only to selected items without general photo-library permission. On iPhone 1.2, the system file picker also lets the user choose supporting documents; Limen copies only the selected files into the record's private evidence storage.
 
 ## Permissions and third-party components
 
 The signed Android release declares the `INTERNET` permission. It also contains `dev.peringer.limen.DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION`, a private permission generated by an Android support library so the app's own components can communicate; it grants no access to device data and cannot be used by other apps.
 
-iOS has no equivalent of an internet permission. The iPhone release declares one usage description, for the camera, which is the text the system shows when it asks.
+iOS has no equivalent of an internet permission. Before iPhone 1.2, Limen declares a camera usage description. iPhone 1.2 also supplies a Face ID usage description for the optional library/settings gate. The operating system performs device-owner authentication; Limen does not receive the user's biometric data or device passcode.
 
 Open-source libraries used by the Android build run inside the app. The iPhone build carries no third-party code at all: it ships a reduced copy of libphonenumber's published metadata and the cantonal flags as data files. Neither build adds advertising, analytics or developer-operated collection services.
 
